From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:26:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: hand the transport what it needs for contacts and
 presence

The webOS IM transport turns libpurple buddies into db8 contacts and group
messages into Messaging app rows. Four things it needs that stock
tdlib-purple does not give it:

- Profile fields. Telegram knows a user's phone number, @username and
  first/last name, but tdlib-purple only shows them in the user-info popup.
  Store them on the buddy's blist node (tdlib-phone, tdlib-username,
  tdlib-first-name, tdlib-last-name; the transport reads these keys
  literally), for new and existing buddies alike, removing a key when the
  field is empty.
- Presence. Upstream maps every status other than online to AWAY, which the
  transport shows as an orange "idle" dot on every contact. Map only
  userStatusRecently ("seen within the last couple of days", the usual
  privacy-limited answer) to AWAY, and everything else - offline, last
  week, last month, hidden - to OFFLINE.
- The sender of a group message. serv_got_chat_in() has a single `who`
  slot, which carries the display name; there is no way to reach the
  sender from it. Send "id<userId>\x1f<display name>" instead when the
  sender is a user; the transport splits on \x1f, using the id as the
  routable address (so the app can open a 1:1 with the sender) and the
  rest as the name.
- Handle-style addressing. webOS reduces a contact's display name to a
  handle - ASCII alphanumerics only, lowercased ("Herman van Hazendonk" ->
  "hermanvanhazendonk") - and may address a conversation by it. Resolve
  such names as a last resort, after display name and @username.

Not for upstream: the blist keys, the \x1f convention and the handle
normalization are contracts with the webOS transport, and other front-ends
would show the raw "id<n>\x1f" prefix in group chats.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [webOS specific]
